==============================================================================
DEEP-DIVE FINDINGS (diagnostic scripts diag1/diag2/diag3)
==============================================================================

KEY STRUCTURAL FACT: In T_G, every EDGE of G is oriented forward in vertex
order (i<j edge => i->j). So the sub-digraph on G's edges is ALWAYS acyclic
(transitive). Strength of T_G can only come from NON-edges (which give the
backward arcs j->i for i<j).

CLEAN CHARACTERIZATION OF "exists-order T_G strong" (n=2..7, NO exception):
  exists-order T_G strongly connected  <=>  G is NEITHER empty NOR complete.
  The ONLY two graphs with exists-strong=FALSE at every n are:
     - EMPTY graph  (no edges -> T_G has no forward arcs of G; transitive)
     - COMPLETE K_n (all edges forward -> T_G = transitive tournament)
  For ALL other graphs, some relabelling makes T_G strong.
  => This is why "exists-order-strong <=> HamCycle" FAILS: K_n is Hamiltonian
     but exists-strong=FALSE (T_{K_n} is always the transitive tournament);
     and many non-Hamiltonian graphs (e.g. with a cut vertex) are non-empty,
     non-complete, hence exists-strong=TRUE. Counts of exStrong=1 & HC=0:
        n=4:7, n=5:25, n=6:107, n=7:660.

CLEAN CHARACTERIZATION OF "exists-order spine+wrap":
  spine+wrap=TRUE  =>  G has a directed Ham cycle in some T_G order = G has a
  Ham cycle WHERE the cycle vertices are consecutive-forward and the wrap is the
  single back-edge. This is STRICTLY STRONGER than "G Hamiltonian":
  HC-but-no-spine+wrap (false negatives): K_n at every n (T_{K_n} transitive,
  wrap (n-1)->0 never forward) PLUS a few self-complementary-type graphs
  (C_5 at n=5, the 6e/9e graphs at n=6, the 7e graph C_7 at n=7).
  False-positive count (spine+wrap but no HC) grows: 1,2,10,43,351 for n=3..7.
  => spine+wrap is NOT equivalent to HamCycle in either direction.

ALCUIN NUMBER vs VERTEX-COVER tau (Csorba-Hurkens-Woeginger bound tau<=Alcuin<=tau+1):
  Confirmed: Alcuin in {tau, tau+1} for ALL graphs, NO "other" at any n.
  Alcuin==tau+1 counts: n=1:1, n=2:1, n=3:1, n=4:2, n=5:4, n=6:8, n=7:20.
  Alcuin==tau   counts: n=1:0, n=2:1, n=3:3, n=4:9, n=5:30, n=6:148, n=7:1024.

IMPLICATIONS THAT HOLD WITH NO COUNTEREXAMPLE UP TO n=7:
  [TASK1] G has Ham PATH  <=>  exists order with T_G spine all-forward.  (1044/1044)
  [bound] tau <= Alcuin <= tau+1.                                        (all graphs)
  [char ] exists-order-T_G-strong  <=>  G neither empty nor complete.    (all graphs)
IMPLICATIONS THAT FAIL (with counterexamples):
  [TASK2a] HamCycle <=> exists-order spine+wrap   (FAILS both directions)
  [TASK2b] HamCycle <=> T_G strong (natural)       (FAILS both directions)
  [TASK2c] HamCycle <=> exists-order T_G strong    (FAILS both directions;
           canonical counterexample = K_n: Hamiltonian but never strong)
